=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The IPv4 default route on pc1's `eth0` interface.

**Mechanism:** pc1 holds the route `default via 195.11.14.254 dev eth0`, but `195.11.14.254` does not exist on the `195.11.14.0/24` segment — the real router (router1) sits at `195.11.14.1`. When pc1 tries to forward any packet destined outside its own subnet (e.g., to pc2's `200.1.1.2`), it ARPs for `195.11.14.254`, receives no reply (the neighbor entry is in `FAILED` state), and immediately generates an ICMP "Destination Host Unreachable" back to itself. This explains why all pings from pc1 fail with that exact message sourced from pc1's own address (`195.11.14.2`). The reverse path (pc2 → pc1) silently times out because router1 correctly delivers the packet to pc1, but pc1's reply must also be sent off-subnet through the non-existent gateway — so no reply ever leaves pc1. The gateway itself (`195.11.14.1`) remains pingable from pc1 because that address is on the directly-attached `/24` subnet and requires no default route lookup.

**Localization:** pc1, `eth0`, default route entry.

**Root cause:** After "cleaning up" network settings, pc1's default gateway was set to `195.11.14.254` instead of the correct `195.11.14.1`, leaving pc1 unable to forward any off-subnet traffic.

**Fix:** On pc1, remove the stale route and install the correct one: `ip route del default; ip route add default via 195.11.14.1 dev eth0`.
=== END ===